# d5-01 — project setup, knowledge curation, custom instructions
projects-setup|5.1|3|An associate is setting up a Project for a recurring analysis and is unsure what belongs in the instructions versus what belongs in each request.|Put what stays constant in the instructions and what changes in the request|Put everything in the instructions so that each request can be as short as possible and users do not need to remember what to include when they come to use it|Put everything into each individual request rather than the instructions|Put the instructions in both places so that they are reinforced and the model is more likely to follow them consistently across the different requests it receives|Configuration holds the invariant part and the request carries the variable part. That division is what makes a Project reusable without re-explaining the task.|Loading everything into instructions leaves no room for the variable input. Putting everything in the request abandons the Project, and duplication creates two places to maintain.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team uploads twelve documents to a Project. Four are current policies and eight are drafts that were never adopted.|Remove the drafts, since knowledge that was never adopted will still be treated as authoritative|Label the drafts clearly so the model can tell which documents represent adopted policy and which were superseded before they were ever put into effect|Keep all twelve of the documents there for the sake of completeness|Add an instruction telling the model to prefer the current policies over the drafts|Everything in project knowledge is treated as available context. Unadopted drafts will be drawn on as readily as policy, so removal is the reliable fix.|Labels and preference instructions both rely on correct selection every time. Completeness is not a virtue when the extra content is wrong.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|A team's project instructions run to three pages and people have noticed that recently added rules seem to have less effect than older ones.|Consolidate around the rules that matter, since a long instruction set dilutes each rule|Move the newer rules to the top of the instructions so that they are encountered first and carry more weight in how the model interprets the whole set|Split the instructions across two Projects|Repeat the newer rules at both the beginning and the end of the instructions|Instructions compete for attention, and a three-page set dilutes everything in it. Consolidating restores the leverage each remaining rule has.|Reordering and repetition redistribute attention within a bloated set. Splitting fragments the workflow rather than simplifying the instructions.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|A Project has accumulated documents over eighteen months and nobody has ever removed one.|Establish a review cadence so knowledge is curated rather than only added to|Remove the documents that are obviously out of date now|Add newer documents so the current material outweighs the older material in the overall body of knowledge the Project holds|Ask people to be more selective about what they upload in future|Project knowledge is a curated set that degrades without maintenance. A review cadence is what turns curation into a process rather than a one-off cleanup.|A one-time cleanup fixes today and leaves the pattern. Adding more material dilutes rather than curates, and asking for selectivity does not address what is already there.
context-limits|5.4|3|An associate notices their Project's answers have become less precise since they added a large reference manual to its knowledge.|Check whether the manual is needed for the questions actually being asked|Move to a model with a larger context window so that the manual can be included|Ask more specific questions of the Project when using it|Split the manual into sections and upload only the section that relates to whatever question is being asked at the time it is needed|More context is not automatically better, and irrelevant material competes with what matters. Checking whether it earns its place is the first question.|A larger window accommodates the problem rather than solving it. More specific questions work around it, and manual section selection is a workaround for an unexamined inclusion.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|A team shares a Project and one member keeps changing the instructions to suit their own preferences.|Agree who owns the configuration and how changes get made|Ask that person to consult the team before making further changes to the shared instructions that everyone else depends on for their own work|Give that person a separate Project of their own|Revert the instructions each time they are changed so that the configuration returns to the version the team originally agreed on together|Shared configuration without an ownership model means every user is an editor. Establishing ownership is the governance fix rather than an interpersonal one.|A request relies on goodwill. A separate Project addresses one person, and reverting is an ongoing conflict rather than a resolution.
organisation|5.4|3|An associate has fourteen Projects and cannot remember which one holds the documents they need.|Adopt a naming and scoping convention so the right Project is identifiable|Search across all fourteen Projects whenever they need to find something|Consolidate everything into one Project|Keep a separate note listing what each of the fourteen Projects contains|Findability comes from consistent naming and clear scope. That scales, whereas memory and external indexes do not.|Searching everywhere is the symptom. Consolidation destroys the isolation Projects provide, and an external index is another thing to maintain.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team's Project answers questions well but occasionally cites a document that was uploaded for a different purpose entirely.|Scope the Project to one purpose so unrelated documents are not in play|Add an instruction telling the model which documents are relevant to which kinds of question|Ask questions that specify which document should be used|Remove the document that is being cited inappropriately|Everything in a Project is in scope for every question. Matching the Project's scope to a single purpose is what prevents cross-contamination structurally.|Routing instructions and per-question specification both rely on correct selection. Removing one document leaves the pattern for the next one.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|A team wants their Project to behave one way for drafting and another way for reviewing, and the instructions currently describe both.|Use two Projects, since standing instructions apply to every response in one|Write conditional instructions describing when each behaviour applies|Ask users to state which of the two modes they want|Alternate the instructions depending on which kind of work the team is doing during any particular week of the month|Standing instructions apply to everything. Two genuinely different behaviours belong in two Projects rather than in a conditional that must be resolved correctly each time.|Conditional instructions ask the model to classify every request. Per-request mode selection puts the burden on users, and alternating serves neither purpose well.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|A Project contains a document that is correct but describes a process the team stopped following six months ago.|Remove it, since a correct description of an abandoned process is still misleading|Keep it as a historical record so the team can refer back to how the process used to work if they ever need to understand a decision made under the old approach|Add a note to the document saying the process is no longer followed|Keep it and add an instruction telling the model that the process described in that document is no longer current and should not be recommended to users|Accuracy about the past is not relevance to the present. A document describing an abandoned process will be drawn on as guidance unless removed.|Historical records belong somewhere other than active knowledge. Notes and instructions both rely on the qualification being applied every time.
context-limits|5.4|3|An associate wants to add a 400-page manual to a Project where questions only ever concern two of its chapters.|Add only the chapters the questions concern|Add the whole manual so that any question can be answered, including ones that fall outside the two chapters people have happened to ask about so far|Add the manual and rely on the model to find the relevant chapters within it|Summarise the manual and add the summary instead of the full document|Curating to what is actually used keeps answers precise and costs less. The two chapters are the evidence of what the Project needs.|Adding everything for hypothetical questions dilutes the material that matters. Relying on retrieval within noise is weaker, and a summary loses the detail questions need.
projects-setup|5.1|3|A team is creating a Project and debating whether to include instructions about tone.|Include it if tone matters for every response the Project produces|Leave tone out of the instructions, since it varies between different pieces of work and is better specified in each individual request when it matters|Include a detailed style guide within the project instructions|Include tone instructions but keep them brief so that they do not take up too much room within the overall set of instructions the Project carries|Configuration is for what applies universally. If tone is constant across the Project's outputs, it belongs there; if it varies, it belongs in the request.|Excluding it by default ignores the case where it is constant. A detailed style guide may be more than needed, and brevity is a formatting concern rather than the criterion.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|A team wants everyone to get the same answers, and each member currently maintains their own copy of the same Project.|Consolidate into one shared Project so there is a single configuration|Ask everyone to keep their copies synchronised so that the configurations stay the same and the answers people receive remain consistent with one another|Nominate one person's copy as authoritative|Compare the copies periodically to identify where they have diverged|Parallel copies diverge. A single shared configuration is what makes consistency structural rather than something to maintain.|Manual synchronisation across copies is exactly what fails. A nominated authoritative copy still leaves others in use, and periodic reconciliation is ongoing work.
organisation|5.4|3|An associate keeps one Project per client and one per internal workstream, and some pieces of work involve both.|Decide which dimension the Project boundary follows and handle the overlap explicitly|Create a third Project for the work that involves both a client and an internal workstream|Put the overlapping work in whichever Project seems more relevant at the time the work is being carried out by whoever happens to be doing it|Merge the client and workstream Projects so that everything is available in one place and the question of where something belongs does not arise|Two organising dimensions that cross produce ambiguity at the intersection. Choosing the primary boundary and defining how overlap is handled resolves it.|A third Project multiplies the problem. Ad-hoc placement makes things unfindable, and merging destroys the isolation.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team's Project gives good answers about the documents it holds and confident wrong answers about topics those documents do not cover.|Instruct it to answer from its documents and to say when they do not cover a question|Add documents covering the additional topics so that the Project's knowledge extends to the questions people are actually bringing to it in practice|Tell colleagues to only ask about covered topics|Ask more specific questions so that the model is more likely to find relevant material|The problem is that the knowledge boundary is invisible. Making the Project declare when it is outside its documents is what stops confident answers from unsupported ground.|Adding documents moves the boundary without making it visible. Telling people the scope relies on them knowing it, and specificity does not prevent the fallback.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|An associate's project instruction says to be concise, and answers have become too brief to be useful.|Say what conciseness applies to and what must be included regardless|Remove the conciseness instruction so that answers return to the length they were before the instruction was added to the Project's configuration|Ask for longer answers within each individual request instead|Add a second instruction saying that answers should also be complete|A global brevity instruction applies to everything including what carries the value. Scoping it and protecting the essentials keeps both properties.|Removing it abandons a real goal. Per-request overrides fight the standing instruction, and stacking a competing adjective leaves the trade-off unresolved.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|A team discovers two documents in their Project that contradict each other on an important point.|Resolve which is correct and remove the other|Add an instruction telling the model which of the two documents takes precedence|Keep both so the model can present both positions|Add a note to each document explaining that there is a conflicting version and that users should check which one applies to their particular situation|Contradictory knowledge produces inconsistent answers. Resolving the conflict at source is the only fix that makes the Project's output reliable.|Precedence rules rely on correct application. Presenting both positions passes the unresolved conflict to users, and notes document the problem without fixing it.
context-limits|5.4|3|A Project's answers are good but noticeably slower than a plain conversation on the same topic.|Check whether all the loaded knowledge is needed for the questions being asked|Accept the slower responses, since the additional context is what makes the answers better than they would be|Move to a faster model tier so responses come back sooner|Ask shorter questions so that the total amount of material being processed on each request is smaller than it currently is for the Project|Loaded context costs time to process on every request. Whether all of it earns its place is the question that addresses the cause.|Accepting it forgoes an easy improvement. A faster tier pays to process possibly unnecessary material, and shorter questions do not reduce the loaded knowledge.
projects-setup|5.1|3|A team is deciding whether a piece of standing guidance belongs in project instructions or in an uploaded document.|Put short standing rules in instructions and reference material in documents|Put everything in documents so that the instructions stay short and everything the Project knows is held consistently in one type of place|Put everything into the instructions rather than into documents|Put it in whichever place has more room available, since the distinction between the two does not make a practical difference to how the Project behaves|Instructions shape behaviour and documents supply reference material. Matching content to that distinction is what makes both work as intended.|Collapsing everything into one type loses the distinction. Choosing by available space treats a design question as a capacity one.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|An associate shares a Project with a colleague who then asks questions it was never configured to answer.|Clarify the Project's scope so its purpose travels with the share|Add documents covering the colleague's questions so that the Project becomes useful for their work as well as for the original purpose|Ask the colleague to use a different Project|Ask the colleague to check with the associate before asking questions|A shared Project needs its scope communicated, otherwise recipients discover the boundary by getting bad answers. Stating it is the fix.|Expanding scope for one colleague changes the Project. Redirecting them and requiring pre-checks both work around the missing scope statement.
organisation|5.4|3|A team's Projects are named after the person who created them rather than what they contain.|Rename them after their purpose so they can be found by what they do|Keep a directory mapping each person's name to the contents of the Projects they created|Ask people to create rather fewer Projects overall|Ask each person to explain what their Projects contain when a colleague needs something from one of them|Names are the primary means of finding things. Purpose-based naming makes Projects discoverable without external lookup or asking someone.|A directory is another artefact to maintain. Fewer Projects does not make them findable, and asking people is a bottleneck.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team uploads a spreadsheet to a Project and finds the model answers from the column headers rather than the data.|Ask questions that require the values and state that answers must come from the rows|Convert the spreadsheet to a document format before uploading it|Reduce the number of columns held in the uploaded spreadsheet|Upload the spreadsheet again in case the original upload did not process the data correctly when it was first added to the Project's knowledge|Headers are salient and self-describing, so a loosely framed question can be answered from them alone. Requiring the answer to rest on the rows forces the model into the data.|Reformatting and trimming columns change presentation without changing what the question demands. Re-uploading addresses a failure that did not occur.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|An associate adds an instruction to their Project and it works, so they add four more the same week.|Test the effect of each addition, since instructions interact rather than accumulate cleanly|Continue adding instructions as needs arise, since each one addresses a real requirement and the set will naturally converge on the behaviour the team wants|Add all five of the instructions and review them on a monthly basis|Order the five instructions by importance so that the most important ones are encountered first and are more likely to be followed by the model|Instructions compete rather than stack, so an addition that helps alone may weaken others. Testing the combined effect is what catches that.|Unchecked accumulation is how instruction sets become diluted. Monthly review is slow feedback, and ordering does not reveal interaction effects.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|A team's Project knowledge includes a document that has been updated externally three times since it was uploaded.|Establish who refreshes uploaded copies and when|Upload the current version now so that the Project reflects the latest state of the document as it exists in the external system today|Add a note saying the document may not be current|Link to the external document instead of uploading a copy|Uploaded copies are snapshots that drift. Assigning refresh ownership is what keeps them current rather than relying on someone noticing.|A one-time refresh fixes today and leaves the pattern. A staleness note documents the problem, and linking may not be supported as a knowledge source.
context-limits|5.4|3|A team wants to add every document their department produces to one Project so nothing is ever missing.|Curate to what questions actually need, since irrelevant material degrades answers|Add everything, since a question could concern any of the documents and having them available means nothing is ever unavailable when it is needed|Add everything and rely on retrieval to find what is relevant|Add everything but organise it into clearly named sections|Precision falls as irrelevant material competes with what matters. Curation is what makes a knowledge base useful rather than merely complete.|Comprehensiveness for hypothetical questions is the misconception. Relying on retrieval within noise is weaker, and organisation does not remove the competition.
projects-setup|5.1|3|An associate is setting up a Project and wants to know how much detail the instructions need.|Enough to remove the decisions you would otherwise repeat in every request|As much as possible, since more detailed instructions leave less room for the model to make choices that do not match what the team is looking for|Include as little detail as possible in the project instructions|Enough to describe the task fully, including background about why the work matters and how it fits into the wider process the team is responsible for|Instructions exist to remove repeated decisions. That criterion tells you what to include and what to leave out.|Maximal detail produces the dilution problem. Minimal instructions leave the repetition, and background context is usually not what shapes behaviour.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|A team member leaves and their Projects held configuration nobody else has a copy of.|Treat Project configuration as shared infrastructure with an owner and a backup|Ask departing team members to hand over their Projects as part of the standard leaving process|Recreate the configuration from scratch|Copy the Projects into a shared location when someone announces they are leaving|Single-owner configuration is an ongoing risk that a departure makes visible. Treating it as shared infrastructure is the durable fix.|A handover step addresses planned departures only. Recreation discards working configuration, and a copy at notice time is the same one-off rescue.
organisation|5.4|3|An associate cannot decide whether to organise Projects by client, by task type, or by team.|Choose the dimension along which context is least shared|Organise by whichever dimension has the most Projects so that each one contains a manageable amount of material within it|Organise by client, since that is the most common approach|Organise by all three dimensions using a naming convention that captures each of them|Project boundaries exist to isolate context. Splitting along the dimension where context is least shared is what makes each Project coherent.|Counting Projects is not a design criterion. Convention for its own sake ignores the specific situation, and encoding three dimensions in names does not create isolation.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team's Project contains a document with information that is correct but that the team is not permitted to share externally.|Check whether the Project's sharing scope matches the document's restrictions|Add an instruction telling the model not to include information from that document in any output that is intended for an external audience|Remove the document from the Project's knowledge altogether|Mark the document as confidential so that anyone using the Project is aware of the restriction that applies to the material it contains|Restricted material in a Project shared beyond its permitted audience is a disclosure risk regardless of instructions. Matching scope to restriction is the control.|Output instructions rely on correct application every time. Removing it may lose needed content, and a marking informs users without restricting access.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|A team's project instructions describe the output format, and the format has changed but the instructions have not.|Update the instructions, since they are the authority for every response|Ask people to specify the new format in their requests until the instructions can be updated at some point when someone has time to do it|Add the new format alongside the old one in the instructions|Ask the model to use the most recent format it has seen|Standing instructions govern every response until changed. Updating them is the direct fix and takes less effort than working around them.|Per-request overrides fight the configuration. Keeping both formats creates ambiguity, and asking the model to infer recency is unreliable.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|An associate wants to know whether their Project's knowledge is still serving its purpose.|Check whether recent questions were answered from the documents or from general knowledge|Ask people whether they are satisfied with the answers so that any problems with the Project's knowledge can be identified from user feedback|Count up how many documents the Project currently holds in total|Compare the current documents against the list of documents that were originally uploaded to see what has been added or changed since the Project was created|Whether answers actually draw on the loaded knowledge is the direct test of whether that knowledge is doing its job.|Satisfaction surveys measure perception. Document counts measure volume, and comparing against the original list tracks changes rather than usefulness.
context-limits|5.4|3|A team notices their Project performs worse on questions that require combining information from two documents than on questions about a single document.|Ask questions that name both documents and the relationship being examined|Move to a model with a larger context window so that both documents can be held in mind more effectively during the course of a single question|Merge the two documents together into a single combined document|Upload the two documents again so that they are positioned next to each other in the Project's knowledge and are more likely to be considered together|Cross-document questions benefit from being framed explicitly, because naming both sources and the relationship directs attention to the comparison rather than to either document alone.|A larger window addresses capacity rather than framing. Merging changes the source material, and upload order is not a meaningful lever.
projects-setup|5.1|3|A team is setting up a Project for work that will be done by people with very different levels of expertise.|Configure for the least expert user, since experts can add detail in their requests|Configure for the most expert user so that the Project's full capability is available and less experienced people can ask for simpler explanations when needed|Create two separate Projects for the two different levels of expertise|Configure for the average level of expertise across the team|Configuring for the least expert user makes the Project usable by everyone, and expertise adds detail through the request rather than requiring different configuration.|Configuring for experts leaves novices stranded. Two Projects doubles maintenance, and an average configuration suits nobody precisely.
sharing-collaboration|5.4|3|An associate shares a Project and colleagues start adding documents to it without telling anyone.|Establish who may add knowledge and how additions are agreed|Ask colleagues to notify the team whenever they add a document|Remove the documents that colleagues have added without asking|Review the Project's contents periodically to identify documents that have been added and decide whether each of them should remain in place|Shared knowledge needs a contribution model. Establishing who may add and how is the governance fix rather than a cleanup.|Notification relies on remembering. Removing additions is a conflict, and periodic review is ongoing cleanup rather than prevention.
organisation|5.4|3|A team's Project works well but is named in a way that means nothing to anyone outside the two people who set it up.|Rename it so its purpose is clear to someone encountering it for the first time|Add a description to the Project explaining what it is for, so that the name can stay as it is|Explain the name to colleagues when they need to use it|Keep the name, since the two people who set it up are the main users and renaming would disrupt their own familiarity with the Project|Names are what people navigate by. A name that only makes sense to its creators is a barrier to everyone else.|A description helps but the name is what appears in lists. Explaining it is a bottleneck, and preserving creator familiarity prioritises two people over everyone else.
project-knowledge|5.1|3|A team's Project holds a policy document, and the questions people ask are mostly about exceptions to that policy.|Add the material covering exceptions, since that is what the questions are actually about|Ask the model to reason about likely exceptions based on the policy document that has been uploaded to the Project's knowledge already|Tell people to ask about the policy instead|Add an instruction saying that exceptions exist and should be considered when answering questions about how the policy applies to particular situations|Project knowledge should match the questions being asked. When exceptions dominate the questions, exception material is what the Project needs.|Asking the model to infer exceptions invites invention. Redirecting the questions ignores the real need, and an instruction about exceptions without the content adds nothing.
custom-instructions|5.3|3|An associate writes a project instruction that works well and wants to know whether to apply it to their other Projects.|Test it in each Project, since instructions interact with the material there|Apply it everywhere, since an instruction that improves output in one Project will produce the same improvement in the others as well|Apply the instruction only to the Projects that are similar|Keep it in the one Project, since instructions are specific to the context they were written for and are unlikely to transfer usefully elsewhere|Instructions interact with a Project's knowledge and purpose, so transfer is an empirical question rather than an assumption in either direction.|Blanket application assumes transfer. Similarity is a weak proxy, and assuming no transfer forgoes a possible improvement without testing.
knowledge-hygiene|5.4|3|A team's Project produces good answers, and nobody knows which of its twenty documents are actually being used.|Check which documents answers draw on so unused material can be removed|Keep all twenty, since the answers are good and removing documents risks degrading the quality that the Project currently delivers to its users|Remove whichever of the documents happen to be the oldest ones|Ask users which documents they think are most useful so that the Project's knowledge can be trimmed based on the feedback they provide|Identifying which documents contribute is what makes curation evidence-based rather than a guess about what might matter.|Keeping everything because it works avoids the question. Age is not usage, and users cannot see which documents answers drew on.
context-limits|5.4|3|A team is deciding whether to put a large body of reference material in a Project or to attach the relevant part to each request.|Base it on whether the same part is needed each time or a different part each time|Put it in the Project, since persistent knowledge is more convenient than attaching material to every individual request that people make|Attach the relevant material to each separate request instead|Put it in the Project and also attach the relevant part to each request|If the same material is needed every time, it belongs in the Project; if a different part is needed each time, attaching is more precise. Usage pattern is the criterion.|Convenience and precision arguments both ignore the usage pattern. Doing both duplicates the material and costs twice.
projects-setup|5.1|3|An associate is asked to set up a Project for a process that is about to change substantially.|Wait until the process settles, or configure only the parts that will not change|Set it up now based on the current process so that people can start using it and adjust the configuration|Set it up based on the expected future process|Set up two Projects, one for the current process and one for the future one|Configuring against a process that is about to change produces rework. Building only the stable parts is what avoids wasted effort.|Configuring the current process guarantees rework. Configuring an unfinalised future process guesses, and two Projects doubles the work.
